;; G4a (docs/upcoming/mutable-globals-plan.md section 4.4): `^atomic ^mut` on a
;; top-level def makes every read a sequentially-consistent load and every `set!` a sequentially-consistent
;; store.
;;
;; WHAT THIS BUYS, precisely:
;;   - no torn read or write;
;;   - no hoisting -- a bare global read in a loop is free to be cached in a
;;     register, so a spinning reader would never observe another thread's
;;     store however atomically that store was made.  This is the practical
;;     one;
;;   - sequentially-consistent ordering with other atomic operations.
;;
;; WHAT IT DOES NOT BUY -- and this is the part worth reading twice:
;; `(set! c (+ c 1))` is a LOAD then a STORE, not an atomic read-modify-write.
;; Two threads running it still lose updates.  `^atomic` makes each half
;; indivisible; it does not fuse them.  A concurrent counter wants a CAS or a
;; fetch-add (stdlib/atomic.tur), or a lock (stdlib/mutex.tur).
;;
;; EIGHT-BYTE SCALARS ONLY -- int, float, cstr, ptr.  The atomics layer's host
;; shim is uint64_t-typed, so the emitted access is 8 bytes whatever the
;; declaration says, and a narrower global really is declared narrower.
;; Admitting :bool produced an 8-byte store into a 1-byte object -- an overflow
;; of the adjacent statics that still printed the right answer.  A bool flag is
;; spelled as an int here for exactly that reason.
;;
;; The float probe uses 7.1, never 7.0: a double crosses the integer-typed
;; atomics layer through its bit pattern, so a truncation bug in that path
;; would be invisible with a whole number.
;;
;; Single-threaded here on purpose.  What a fixture can assert cheaply is that
;; the lowering is correct and round-trips every scalar kind; that two threads
;; sharing one without tearing is *observed* rather than asserted is the nature
;; of the property, and a racing fixture would be flaky rather than rigorous.
(def ^atomic ^mut counter 0)
(def ^atomic ^mut ratio   1.5)
(def ^atomic ^mut flag    0)      ; an int flag: :bool is one byte, see below
(def ^atomic ^mut label   "start")

(defn bump [] : void
  (set! counter (+ counter 1)))

(defn main [] : int
  (bump)
  (bump)
  (bump)
  (println counter)
  (set! ratio 7.1)
  (println ratio)
  (set! flag 1)
  (println flag)
  (set! label "done")
  (println label)
  0)
